#{[{:activity/id #uuid "08c34207-4158-5cd6-b7c7-eda8eb60b32c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158461 :tutorial/content "We're used to plotting curves where $y$ is a function of $x$. However, we can also plot curves where $x$ is a function of $y$.\n\nFor example, consider the curve $x = y^2$. To graph this curve, we can start by calculating the values of $x$ for some values of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y =-3 & ⟶x = (-3)^{2} = 9 \\\\ y =-2 & ⟶x = (-2)^{2} = 4 \\\\ y =-1 & ⟶x = (-1)^{2} = 1 \\\\ y = 0 & ⟶x = 0^{2} = 0 \\\\ y = 1 & ⟶x = 1^{2} = 1 \\\\ y = 2 & ⟶x = 2^{2} = 4 \\\\ y = 3 & ⟶x = 3^{2} = 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize these results in the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n(9,-3), \\quad (4,-2), \\quad (1,-1), \\quad (0,0), \\quad (1,1), \\quad (4,2), \\quad (9,3)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2978/Source/Images/ac34c9f656f2cc029ebe8b8d3872924c.png)\n\nWe say that the equation of the curve is defined **implicitly**, because instead of the usual $y = f(x)$, we have $x = f(y)$.\n\n**Watch out!** Here, we are viewing $x=y^2$ not as a function but rather as an equation. A point belongs to the curve if and only if its coordinates satisfy the equation. The curve does not represent a function since it fails the vertical line test." :tutorial/id #uuid "3217d90b-5727-5445-a812-db001667675c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4707 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ad692cd2-b2aa-553b-af11-d2a2e96f2938" :step/math-academy-id 29069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158165}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158165 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157286 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157100} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109b0bfb-10fe-5b31-adb2-be746ef71936" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050774}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157899}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting X as a Function of Y Using a Table of Values"} :step/id #uuid "97eff80c-79f1-5938-b354-b7a812934467" :step/math-academy-id 29070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158166 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157287 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157101} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51ae30bd-569c-52dd-96a0-4244578db5bf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048670}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157901}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting X as a Function of Y Given the Graph of the Corresponding Function"} :step/id #uuid "8a56a63c-886a-5511-8e90-a5a786cbac97" :step/math-academy-id 29071 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158167}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157288 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157102} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2981f6-bfbf-5c1e-a4a2-2814dc24804a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049228}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157900}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Quadratic Function Where X Is a Function of Y"} :step/id #uuid "4dfeda37-1f1e-58ac-897c-5bb43d56241a" :step/math-academy-id 29072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158462 :tutorial/content "Another way to plot $x = f(y)$ is to first plot the corresponding function $y = f(x)$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x$.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this technique to plot the curve $x=y^2$. To get the corresponding function, we swap $x$ and $y$ and get $y=x^2$. This curve is familiar and much easier to graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2978/Source/Images/61a79246c46a57d8b1aa3d107206fdc6.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=y^2$, we just have to reflect the curve $y=x^2$ over the line $y=x$, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2978/Source/Images/04afecb8450c42675b6307b61aacc623.png)\n\nYou may notice that this process is very similar to plotting the inverse of a function. In fact, if $y=f(x)$ is a one-to-one function, then the corresponding function $x = f(y)$ will always give the inverse function." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef9c9a8e-f3c3-57cf-936a-e4586282e650" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4708 :tutorial/title "Plotting X as a Function of Y Using a Reflection"} :step/id #uuid "fe2be374-df26-589e-a401-2e869139d14d" :step/math-academy-id 29075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158166}}] :activity/title "Plotting X as a Function of Y"}]}
